/doc/existential-risk/1985-hofstadter
<em>Metamagical Themas</em>: Sanity and Survival
Douglas Hofstadter
2012-04-16
2019-04-01

existential-risk philosophy/ethics politics sociology
<figure><img class="float-right page-thumbnail invert-not " height="0" width="0" src="/doc/existential-risk/1985-hofstadter-guillotine.png" title="Humorous cartoon illustrating the dilemma of mutually-assured destruction via nuclear bombs: neither man wants to use the guillotine on the other, but they also can’t get out of the guillotines either." alt=""></figure><div class="page-description-annotation">
<p>3 essays by AI researcher Douglas Hofstadter exploring cooperation/game theory/‘superrationality’ in the context of the failure of political coordination to prevent global nuclear war (published 1985, from <em>Scientific American</em>).</p>
</div>
<p>The following 3 essays were prepared from <a href="/doc/existential-risk/1985-hofstadter-sanityandsurvival.pdf" id="hofstadter-1985-superrationality-pdf" data-link-icon="pdf" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-link-icon-color="#f40f02" data-filesize-bytes="632571" data-filesize-percentage="64" title="&#39;Metamagical Themas: Sanity and Survival&#39;, Hofstadter 1985">pages 737–780</a> of an ebook of <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metamagical_Themas" id="wp-metamagicalthemas" class="link-annotated-partial link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metamagical_Themas#bodyContent" title="‘&lt;em&gt;Metamagical Themas: Questing for the Essence of Mind and Pattern&lt;/em&gt;’, Hofstadter 1982">Metamagical Themas</a>: Questing for the Essence of Mind and Pattern</em> (<span class="date-range">1985<sub><span title="1985 was 41 years ago.">41ya</span></sub></span>) by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_Hofstadter" id="_qcsn6MY5" class="link-annotated-partial link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douglas_Hofstadter#bodyContent" title="Douglas Hofstadter">Douglas Hofstadter</a>, an anthology of articles &amp; essays primarily published in <em><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_American" id="_rKxsy3-n" class="link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientific_American#bodyContent" title="Scientific American">Scientific American</a></em> “between January <span class="date-range">1981<sub><span title="1981 was 45 years ago.">45ya</span></sub></span> and July 1983”. (I omit one entry in “Sanity and Survival”, the essay “The Tumult of Inner Voices, or, What is the Meaning of the Word ‘I’?”, which is unconnected to the other entries on cooperation/<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decision_theory#Choice_under_uncertainty" id="_EAr5mkWS" class="link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decision_theory#bodyContent" title="Decision theory § Choice under uncertainty">decision theory</a>/nuclear war.) All hyperlinks are my insertion.</p>
<p>They are interesting for introducing the idea of ‘<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superrationality" id="_ADe-sl8N" class="link-annotated-partial link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superrationality#bodyContent" title="Superrationality">superrationality</a>’ in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_theory" id="_N6DlYslk" class="link-annotated-partial link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_theory#bodyContent" title="Game theory">game theory</a>, an attempt to devise a decision theory/algorithm for agents which can reach global utility maxima on problems like the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prisoner%27s_dilemma" id="_stG5OymW" class="link-annotated-partial link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prisoner%27s_dilemma#bodyContent" title="Prisoner’s dilemma">prisoner’s dilemma</a> even in the absence of coercion or communication which has partially inspired later decision theories like <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/w/updateless-decision-theory" id="_yLuugKrE" class="link-live" data-link-icon="LW" data-link-icon-type="text" data-link-icon-color="#7faf83" data-url-iframe="https://www.greaterwrong.com/w/updateless-decision-theory?format=preview&amp;theme=classic" title="Updateless Decision Theory">UDT</a> or <a href="https://www.lesswrong.com/w/timeless-decision-theory" id="_3KqT_gG7" class="link-live" data-link-icon="LW" data-link-icon-type="text" data-link-icon-color="#7faf83" data-url-iframe="https://www.greaterwrong.com/w/timeless-decision-theory?format=preview&amp;theme=classic" title="Timeless Decision Theory">TDT</a>, linking decision theory to cooperation (eg. <a href="/doc/www/longtermrisk.org/3451595467c7ec8000d08dc91b2f0bfeb0015363.pdf" id="oesterheld-2018" class="link-live link-annotated" data-link-icon="pdf" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-link-icon-color="#f40f02" data-url-archive="/doc/www/longtermrisk.org/3451595467c7ec8000d08dc91b2f0bfeb0015363.pdf" data-url-original="https://longtermrisk.org/files/Multiverse-wide-Cooperation-via-Correlated-Decision-Making.pdf" data-filesize-bytes="806921" data-filesize-percentage="44" title="Multiverse-wide Cooperation via Correlated Decision Making"><span class="cite"><span class="cite-author">Oesterheld</span><span class="cite-date">2017</span></span></a>) &amp; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_catastrophic_risk#Defining_existential_risks" id="_YHP9Fmrk" class="link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_catastrophic_risk#bodyContent" title="Global catastrophic risk § Defining existential risks">existential risks</a> (specifically, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_warfare" id="_Lfy8I-3W" class="link-live" data-link-icon="wikipedia" data-link-icon-type="svg" data-url-iframe="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_warfare#bodyContent" title="Nuclear warfare">nuclear warfare</a>), and <a href="/doc/www/web.mit.edu/abdc683067290d7f1476714ed2d141f7f2224d33.html" id="_eMiXgvwU" class="link-live" data-link-icon="MIT" data-link-icon-type="text,tri,mono" data-url-archive="/doc/www/web.mit.edu/abdc683067290d7f1476714ed2d141f7f2224d33.html" data-url-original="https://web.mit.edu/remy/" data-filesize-bytes="5164544" data-filesize-percentage="83" title="TCP ex Machina: Computer-Generated Congestion Control">one networking project</a>.</p>
<p>to hide apparatus like the links, you can use reader-mode (<span class="reader-mode-selector-inline"><!-- non-empty span placeholder --></span>).</p>
